Chapter: FireworksSimKernel
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceEffect
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath

 One GPU thread per particle. Each thread reads its particle out of the
 state buffer, integrates it under gravity, air drag, a wind field and a
 per-particle turbulence, ages it, and writes back both the new state and
 a packed render record that the draw pass reads as one instance.

 Positions and velocities live in the state buffer as integers in 1/256
 of a pixel. The physics runs in f32 and converts at the buffer boundary,
 so the state stays a single array<i32> and the motion is still smooth.

 Shells (kind 2) are the exception: the host integrates those itself so
 it knows the exact frame and place to detonate, and this kernel passes
 their state through untouched and only shades them.

 We say:

Section: Kinds

 1 spark (peony), 2 shell, 3 willow, 4 glitter, 5 smoke, 6 ascent trail.
 0 is a free slot.

  fw-k-dead : Integer = 0
  fw-k-spark : Integer = 1
  fw-k-shell : Integer = 2
  fw-k-willow : Integer = 3
  fw-k-glitter : Integer = 4
  fw-k-smoke : Integer = 5
  fw-k-trail : Integer = 6

Section: Random

 fract(sin(x) * 43758.5453), the shader hash, chosen because the WGSL plug
 lowers real-sin to a GPU builtin.

 DeviceMath has no floor, and real-to-int truncates toward zero, which is floor
 only for a positive argument. The hash's significand is negative half the
 time, so the correction below is the whole reason this is not one line: a
 truncating fract returns a negative number for half its inputs, and the
 twinkle it feeds would go dark rather than wrong, which is the harder failure
 to see.

  fw-floor : Real -> Real
  fw-floor (x) =
    let t = real-from-int (real-to-int x)
    in if t > x then t - 1.0 else t

  fw-fract : Real -> Real
  fw-fract (x) = x - fw-floor x

  fw-rand : Real -> Real
  fw-rand (x) = fw-fract (real-sin (x * 12.9898) * 43758.5453)

Section: Air

 Drag is a per-frame velocity multiplier and gravity is in pixels per frame
 squared, at the fixed 60 Hz the host steps this kernel at.

 Both numbers are set against the LIFETIME, not by feel. A star lives 60 to
 200 frames, so a drag of 0.972 leaves 0.972^130 = 2.6% of the launch speed:
 the horizontal component is gone within half a second while gravity keeps
 accumulating, and a burst that should open into a sphere collapses into a
 vertical streak. Terminal speed is grav / (1 - drag), and that is the number
 to check: at 0.045 and 0.990 a spark settles at 4.5 px per frame, which is a
 fall the eye reads as gravity rather than as rain.

  fw-drag : Integer -> Real
  fw-drag (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-shell then 0.999
    else if kind == fw-k-willow then 0.984
    else if kind == fw-k-glitter then 0.988
    else if kind == fw-k-smoke then 0.960
    else if kind == fw-k-trail then 0.938
    else 0.990

  fw-grav : Integer -> Real
  fw-grav (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-smoke then 0.0 - 0.012
    else if kind == fw-k-willow then 0.038
    else if kind == fw-k-glitter then 0.050
    else if kind == fw-k-trail then 0.026
    else 0.045

  fw-wind-take : Integer -> Real
  fw-wind-take (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-smoke then 2.4
    else if kind == fw-k-willow then 1.2
    else if kind == fw-k-glitter then 0.8
    else 0.55

 Turbulence keeps a burst's stars from moving in lockstep: a slow
 sinusoidal field sampled at the particle's own position, phase-shifted
 by its own hash so neighbours disagree.

  fw-turb : Real, Real, Real, Real -> Real
  fw-turb (fg) (px) (py) (t) =
    real-sin (px * 0.0130 + t * 0.0210 + fg * 6.2832) * 0.0240
    + real-sin (py * 0.0074 - t * 0.0163 + fg * 3.1416) * 0.0155

Section: Integration

  fw-new-vx : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer, Real -> Real
  fw-new-vx (vx) (px) (py) (fg) (t) (kind) (wind) =
    (vx + wind * fw-wind-take kind + fw-turb fg px py t) * fw-drag kind

  fw-new-vy : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  fw-new-vy (vy) (px) (py) (fg) (t) (kind) =
    (vy + fw-grav kind + fw-turb (fg + 0.37) py px t * 0.55) * fw-drag kind

Section: Colour

  fw-red : Integer -> Real
  fw-red (c) = real-from-int (c / 65536) / 255.0

  fw-green : Integer -> Real
  fw-green (c) = real-from-int (c / 256 - (c / 65536) * 256) / 255.0

  fw-blue : Integer -> Real
  fw-blue (c) = real-from-int (c - (c / 256) * 256) / 255.0

  fw-sat8 : Real -> Integer
  fw-sat8 (v) = real-to-int (real-max 0.0 (real-min 255.0 (v * 255.0)))

  fw-pack : Real, Real, Real -> Integer
  fw-pack (r) (g) (b) = fw-sat8 r * 65536 + fw-sat8 g * 256 + fw-sat8 b

 A star is white hot at birth and cools to its own colour, then past it
 into the deep red an ember goes out at. `hot` is the whiteness and
 `cool` pulls the blue channel down late in the life so the last of the
 tail reads as ember rather than as a dim version of the shell colour.

 The white window is short on purpose. At a threshold of 0.72 a willow star,
 which lives 200 frames, is white for its first 56 of them, and since those are
 also its brightest frames the shell reads as white and its colour only appears
 once it is too dim to see. Colour has to arrive while the star is still bright.

  fw-hot : Real -> Real
  fw-hot (fade) =
    let a = (fade - 0.90) / 0.10
    in if a < 0.0 then 0.0 else a * a * 0.72

  fw-cool : Real -> Real
  fw-cool (fade) = real-min 1.0 (fade * 2.6)

  fw-shade-r : Integer, Real -> Real
  fw-shade-r (col) (fade) =
    let h = fw-hot fade
    in fw-red col * (1.0 - h) + h

  fw-shade-g : Integer, Real -> Real
  fw-shade-g (col) (fade) =
    let h = fw-hot fade
    in (fw-green col * (1.0 - h) + h * 0.96) * (0.34 + 0.66 * fw-cool fade)

  fw-shade-b : Integer, Real -> Real
  fw-shade-b (col) (fade) =
    let h = fw-hot fade
    in (fw-blue col * (1.0 - h) + h * 0.86) * (0.10 + 0.90 * fw-cool fade)

Section: Brightness and Size

 DECAY HAS TO START AT ONCE AND THEN TAKE ITS TIME, and a power curve cannot
 do both. Any fade^k starts FLAT: its slope at full life is zero for k above
 one, so a star holds its opening brightness, and every value over 1.0 clips
 to the same white through the tone map besides, which widens that plateau
 further. Then the curve falls off a cliff near the end and a whole burst,
 whose stars were dealt lifetimes within a few frames of each other, goes out
 together. That is the "sits there forever and then disappears in chunks".

 So brightness is a decaying EXPONENTIAL in elapsed life rather than a power
 of remaining life, approximated here with the rational 1/(1 + a*e + b*e*e)
 because there is no exp in the device math. It leaves full brightness on the
 first frame, is down to a third by a quarter of the way through, and still
 has a visible tail at the end, so nothing pops out. The peak sits under 1.0
 on purpose: at 2.6 the first third of every life clipped white and no decay
 in it could be seen at all.

 Glitter crackles: a fast hash of the particle and the frame gates it
 almost off and then flares it well past one, which is what makes a
 crackling shell read as crackling rather than as a dimmer peony. The
 render pass takes the intensity as an HDR multiplier, so values above
 the 1.0 mark are the ones the bloom pass picks up.

  fw-twinkle : Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  fw-twinkle (fg) (t) (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-glitter then
      let r = fw-rand (fg * 91.7 + fw-floor (t * 0.5) * 0.731)
      in if r > 0.62 then 1.0 + r * 2.4 else 0.10 + r * 0.25
    else if kind == fw-k-willow then
      0.72 + 0.28 * fw-rand (fg * 13.1 + fw-floor (t * 0.34) * 0.417)
    else 1.0

 decay-at is 1/(1 + a*e + b*e*e) where e is elapsed life, 0 at birth and 1 at
 death. `a` sets how fast it leaves full brightness and `b` how hard the tail
 is pulled down at the end.

  fw-decay : Real, Real, Real -> Real
  fw-decay (fade) (a) (b) =
    let e = 1.0 - fade
    in 1.0 / (1.0 + a * e + b * e * e)

  fw-bright : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> Real
  fw-bright (fade) (fg) (t) (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-dead then 0.0
    else if kind == fw-k-shell then 0.95 + 0.30 * fw-rand (fg + fw-floor (t * 0.5) * 0.11)
    else if kind == fw-k-smoke then 0.030 * fade
    else if kind == fw-k-trail then 0.80 * fw-decay fade 7.0 26.0
    else
      let base = 0.92 * fw-decay fade 3.4 9.0
      in let birth = if fade > 0.985 then 0.9 else 0.0
      in (base + birth) * fw-twinkle fg t kind

  fw-size : Real, Integer -> Real
  fw-size (fade) (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-dead then 0.0
    else if kind == fw-k-shell then 2.6
    else if kind == fw-k-smoke then 9.0 + 30.0 * (1.0 - fade)
    else if kind == fw-k-trail then 0.55 + 1.30 * fade
    else if kind == fw-k-willow then 1.15 + 1.55 * fade
    else if kind == fw-k-glitter then 0.95 + 1.30 * fade
    else 1.45 + 2.30 * fade

Section: Ageing

  fw-fade : Integer, Integer -> Real
  fw-fade (life) (maxl) =
    if maxl <= 0 then 0.0
    else real-max 0.0 (real-min 1.0 (real-from-int life / real-from-int maxl))

  fw-next-life : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fw-next-life (life) (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-dead then 0 else life - 1

  fw-next-kind : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fw-next-kind (life) (kind) =
    if kind == fw-k-dead then fw-k-dead
    else if life <= 1 then fw-k-dead
    else kind

Section: Packing the Render Record

 Four integers per particle. The draw pass takes one instance per slot:
 x and y in 1/16 of a pixel so a star can sit between pixels, the shaded
 colour, and then size in 1/8 of a pixel, intensity in 1/32 of a unit
 (so it reaches 8.0), and the kind, which selects the sprite profile.

  fw-out-pos : Real -> Integer
  fw-out-pos (v) = real-to-int (v * 16.0)

  fw-out-attr : Real, Real, Integer -> Integer
  fw-out-attr (size) (bright) (kind) =
    let s = real-to-int (real-max 0.0 (real-min 255.0 (size * 8.0)))
    in let b = real-to-int (real-max 0.0 (real-min 255.0 (bright * 32.0)))
    in s * 65536 + b * 256 + kind

Section: The Kernel

  fw-sim-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device] Integer
  fw-sim-step (state) (out) (frame) (wind) (gid) = act
    ipx <- device-load state (gid * 8)
    ipy <- device-load state (gid * 8 + 1)
    ivx <- device-load state (gid * 8 + 2)
    ivy <- device-load state (gid * 8 + 3)
    life <- device-load state (gid * 8 + 4)
    maxl <- device-load state (gid * 8 + 5)
    col <- device-load state (gid * 8 + 6)
    kind <- device-load state (gid * 8 + 7)
    let t = real-from-int frame
    in let fg = fw-rand (real-from-int gid * 0.0173)
    in let wf = real-from-int wind / 1000.0
    in let px = real-from-int ipx / 256.0
    in let py = real-from-int ipy / 256.0
    in let vx = real-from-int ivx / 256.0
    in let vy = real-from-int ivy / 256.0
    in let held = if kind == fw-k-shell then 1 else if kind == fw-k-dead then 1 else 0
    in let nvx = if held == 1 then vx else fw-new-vx vx px py fg t kind wf
    in let nvy = if held == 1 then vy else fw-new-vy vy px py fg t kind
    in let npx = if held == 1 then px else px + nvx
    in let npy = if held == 1 then py else py + nvy
    in let nlife = if held == 1 then life else fw-next-life life kind
    in let nkind = if held == 1 then kind else fw-next-kind life kind
    in let fade = fw-fade life maxl
    in let bright = fw-bright fade fg t kind
    in act
      s0 <- device-store state (gid * 8) (real-to-int (npx * 256.0))
      s1 <- device-store state (gid * 8 + 1) (real-to-int (npy * 256.0))
      s2 <- device-store state (gid * 8 + 2) (real-to-int (nvx * 256.0))
      s3 <- device-store state (gid * 8 + 3) (real-to-int (nvy * 256.0))
      s4 <- device-store state (gid * 8 + 4) nlife
      s5 <- device-store state (gid * 8 + 7) nkind
      o0 <- device-store out (gid * 4) (fw-out-pos npx)
      o1 <- device-store out (gid * 4 + 1) (fw-out-pos npy)
      o2 <- device-store out (gid * 4 + 2) (fw-pack (fw-shade-r col fade) (fw-shade-g col fade) (fw-shade-b col fade))
      device-store out (gid * 4 + 3) (fw-out-attr (fw-size fade kind) bright kind)
    end
  end

